                                                    <div id="Button4" class="tabcontent">
                                                        <p class="facpara">A deep and enduring passion for the
                                                            discipline of Marketing is what brought this faculty member
                                                            to the classroom — and it is that same passion that
                                                            continues to shape every session delivered. With a strong
                                                            practitioner orientation, the approach to teaching Marketing
                                                            at GIMS is founded on a single, firmly held conviction: a
                                                            concept that cannot be connected to the real world has
                                                            limited value in a management classroom. This belief is not
                                                            incidental to the pedagogy — it is its foundation. The
                                                            objective, at all times, is to develop in PGDM students not
                                                            merely a familiarity with marketing frameworks, but the
                                                            professional instinct to apply them with confidence,
                                                            precision, and commercial awareness.
                                                        </p>
                                                        <p class="factitle">
                                                            Pedagogical Approach & Classroom Innovations

                                                        </p>
                                                        <p class="facpara">Drawing on industry experience and a
                                                            practitioner's lens, the instructional methodology employed
                                                            is deliberately designed to bridge the gap between
                                                            theoretical knowledge and professional application. The
                                                            classroom is structured as an active, industry-facing
                                                            learning environment, anchored by the following innovations:
                                                        </p>
                                                        <ul class="facul">
                                                            <li><strong>Storytelling with Data:</strong> Rooted in the
                                                                practitioner belief that insight without communication
                                                                is incomplete, every concept is introduced through a
                                                                real-world business narrative before any framework or
                                                                model is presented. Students are first asked to engage
                                                                with a market situation — how a leading food-technology
                                                                brand justifies its loyalty offering, or why a consumer
                                                                goods company restructures its segmentation strategy —
                                                                before the underlying theory is revealed. This
                                                                sequencing ensures that students understand not just
                                                                what a concept is, but why it matters and how it is
                                                                applied in practice.
                                                            </li>
                                                            <li><strong>Live Datasets from Industry: </strong>Reflecting
                                                                the practitioner's insistence that students encounter
                                                                the world as it actually is, sessions are built around
                                                                real datasets drawn from prominent Indian and global
                                                                organisations across e-commerce, fast-moving consumer
                                                                goods, food technology, and telecommunications. Students
                                                                work with authentic, unstructured data — confronting its
                                                                complexity and ambiguity — rather than the clean,
                                                                pre-processed examples found in textbooks. This
                                                                deliberate choice prepares students for the analytical
                                                                realities of professional marketing roles.</li>
                                                            <li><strong>Hands-On Software Training:</strong> Industry
                                                                tools — including Microsoft Excel, R, Python, and CRM
                                                                platforms — are integrated directly into classroom
                                                                sessions. Students do not study these tools in the
                                                                abstract; they use them in real time to clean data,
                                                                build analytical models, and generate outputs that
                                                                mirror those expected in professional settings. The aim
                                                                is to ensure that students leave not as observers of
                                                                marketing technology, but as capable and confident users
                                                                of it.
                                                            </li>
                                                            <li><strong>Case-Based Learning:</strong> Cases are selected
                                                                not for their neatness, but for their relevance and
                                                                complexity. Students are required to move beyond
                                                                descriptive analysis — they are expected to critically
                                                                interrogate the marketing decisions embedded in each
                                                                case, identify what was missed or mishandled, and
                                                                construct and defend alternative courses of action. This
                                                                rigorous engagement with real organisational dilemmas
                                                                develops both the analytical depth and the managerial
                                                                confidence that industry demands.
                                                            </li>
                                                            <li><strong>Gamification and Competitive
                                                                    Assessments:</strong> Timed quizzes and gamified
                                                                exercises are woven into the trimester to reinforce
                                                                retention and replicate the speed of real-world
                                                                decision-making. These formats reward both conceptual
                                                                clarity and applied thinking, and reflect the
                                                                practitioner's understanding that marketing
                                                                professionals are rarely afforded the luxury of extended
                                                                deliberation — agility of thought is as important as
                                                                depth of knowledge.
                                                            </li>
                                                        </ul>

                                                        <p class="factitle">Contribution to Student Learning
                                                        </p>
                                                        <p class="facpara">The contribution of this pedagogical approach
                                                            is most clearly observed in the transformation that takes
                                                            place within the classroom itself. Students who begin the
                                                            trimester as passive recipients of marketing theory
                                                            progressively develop into active, analytical thinkers who
                                                            are capable of interrogating real data, constructing
                                                            evidence-based arguments, and presenting recommendations
                                                            with the rigour and confidence of professional
                                                            practitioners. Through sustained engagement with live
                                                            industry datasets, hands-on software tools, and complex case
                                                            discussions, students build a working vocabulary of
                                                            marketing practice — not as an academic exercise, but as a
                                                            professional capability. The classroom, in this sense,
                                                            functions as a rehearsal space for the real world. For a
                                                            practitioner-educator who believes that every concept must
                                                            earn its place by connecting to reality, the most meaningful
                                                            measure of success is a student who no longer needs to be
                                                            told why marketing matters — because they have experienced,
                                                            firsthand, what it looks like when it is done well.
                                                        </p>
